@*
    The colour this empire flies on the galaxy map.

    Kept in the flag's third colour slot, which is where the game keeps it, but it is not a flag
    colour and it is not edited beside them. The evidence is in the art: a background is three shapes
    packed into one file's red, green and blue channels, each tinted with one of the first three
    colours - and across all sixty-three backgrounds the game ships, the brightest pixel in any blue
    channel is 11 of 255. Nothing is drawn there. Yet the game has a word for the third colour, and
    four of its own empires set it to something distinctive on backgrounds with no blue at all.

    Left unset the game picks one from the flag's primary, which is what almost every empire does -
    thirty-two of the game's fifty-three ready-made empires write nothing here and seventeen more
    write black. So the tile for it says Automatic rather than describing the fallback: what it
    means is that nobody has chosen, and the game will.

    Drawn in the map colours rather than the flag colours, because that is the pair the game keeps
    for every name and this is the half that will be on screen. "Red" is not the same red in both.
*@

<div class="sem-swatches">
    @*
        Following the flag is the ordinary answer, so it is a tile at the front rather than a way of
        undoing one - most empires want it and it should not need discovering.
    *@
    <button type="button" class="sem-swatch none @(Chosen is null ? "selected" : null)"
            title="Automatic" aria-label="Automatic"
            aria-pressed="@(Chosen is null ? "true" : "false")"
            @onclick="() => Set(null)"></button>

    @foreach (var color in Session.Data.Database.FlagColors)
    {
        <button type="button"
                class="sem-swatch @(color.Key == Chosen ? "selected" : null)"
                style="background:rgb(@color.MapRed,@color.MapGreen,@color.MapBlue)"
                title="@Localizer.Prettify(color.Key)"
                aria-pressed="@(color.Key == Chosen ? "true" : "false")"
                @onclick="() => Set(color.Key)"></button>
    }
</div>

@code {
    [CascadingParameter]
    public required DesignSession Session { get; set; }

    private EmpireDesign Design => Session.Current!;

    /// <summary>What the empire flies, or nothing where it follows its flag.</summary>
    private string? Chosen => Design.Flag.MapColor;

    private void Set(string? value) => Session.Edit(d => d.Flag.MapColor = value);
}
